      body:
        "A **tropical cyclone** is a huge, rotating storm system with very **low air pressure** at its centre, winds of at least 119 km/h (when fully developed), and torrential rain. It is the same weather system everywhere, but it has three local names:\n\n- **Hurricane** — North Atlantic, Caribbean and north-east Pacific (e.g. USA, Mexico)\n- **Typhoon** — north-west Pacific (e.g. Philippines, Japan, China)\n- **Cyclone** — Indian Ocean and South Pacific (e.g. Bangladesh, India, Australia)\n\n**Conditions needed to form:**\n\n- **Warm sea** of at least **26.5°C**, down to about 50 m deep — warm water provides the heat and moisture that power the storm.\n- **Latitude roughly 5–30° north or south** of the Equator. Within about 5° of the Equator the **Coriolis effect** (the deflection caused by the Earth's rotation) is too weak to make the storm spin — which is one reason Singapore (1°N) almost never gets hit.\n- **Low wind shear** — winds at different heights blowing at similar speeds and directions, so the storm's tall tower is not torn apart.\n- Usually **late summer and autumn**, when sea temperatures are highest.\n\n**How it forms (step by step):**\n\n- The sun heats the ocean; warm, moist air **rises** quickly, creating an area of **low pressure** at the surface.\n- As the air rises it cools and **condenses** to form towering cumulonimbus clouds. Condensation releases **latent heat**, which warms the air further so it rises even faster — a self-feeding 'engine'.\n- Air rushes in across the sea surface to replace the rising air. The **Coriolis effect** makes this inflowing air spiral — **anticlockwise** in the Northern Hemisphere, **clockwise** in the Southern Hemisphere.\n- At the top (about 12–15 km up) the air flows outwards. Some sinks in the middle, forming the **eye**.\n- The storm is carried along by prevailing winds. When it reaches land (**landfall**) or cooler water, it loses its supply of warm moist air and gradually weakens — but by then it may already have caused huge damage.\n\n**Structure:**\n\n- The **eye**: the calm centre, typically 20–60 km wide, with **sinking air**, light winds, few clouds and the lowest pressure.\n- The **eye wall**: a ring of towering cloud around the eye with the **strongest winds** and **heaviest rain**.\n- **Rain bands**: spiral bands of cloud and rain extending hundreds of kilometres outwards.\n\nThe **Saffir–Simpson scale** rates hurricane strength by sustained wind speed: **Category 1** (119–153 km/h) to **Category 5** (252 km/h or more). Category 3 and above are called 'major' storms. Many typhoon agencies use similar 'super typhoon' labels.",
