      heading: "Singapore's weather: monsoons, Sumatra squalls and haze",
      body:
        "Singapore lies about **1°N** of the Equator, so it is hot and humid all year, with frequent afternoon thunderstorms caused by **convection** (the sun heats the ground, warm moist air rises, cools and condenses into towering cumulonimbus clouds). Singapore has one of the highest rates of **lightning** in the world.\n\nThe year is shaped by two **monsoons** — seasonal reversals of the prevailing wind:\n\n- **Northeast Monsoon (about December to early March)**: winds blow from the north-east across the South China Sea. December–January is the **wet phase**, with long spells of heavy, widespread rain (December is usually the wettest month) and occasional flash floods. Late January–February is the **dry phase**: windy, sunny and the driest time of year.\n- **Southwest Monsoon (about June to September)**: winds blow from the south-west. Generally drier, but with **Sumatra squalls** in the early morning.\n- **Inter-monsoon periods (about April–May and October–November)**: light, variable winds and afternoon/evening thunderstorms — the most lightning.\n\n**Sumatra squalls** are lines of thunderstorms (squall lines) that form at night over **Sumatra** or the **Strait of Malacca** and are blown east towards Singapore, arriving mostly **before dawn or in the early morning**. They bring sudden gusts of about 40–80 km/h, heavy rain for an hour or two, and can fell trees.\n\n**Haze** is a smoky, polluted air condition caused by forest and **peatland fires**, mostly in **Sumatra and Kalimantan (Indonesia)**, where land is cleared by burning for oil palm and pulpwood plantations. During the Southwest Monsoon the winds carry the smoke towards Singapore. Air quality is measured by the **Pollutant Standards Index (PSI)**: above 100 is 'unhealthy', above 200 'very unhealthy', above 300 '**hazardous**'.\n\n**The 2015 haze** was one of the worst ever. A strong **El Niño** made the region unusually dry, so fires burned for months. In late September 2015 the PSI rose into the hazardous range (above 300), and on **25 September 2015** all primary and secondary schools in Singapore were closed. Researchers estimated that the haze may have contributed to around 100,000 early deaths across Indonesia, Malaysia and Singapore. Singapore's **Transboundary Haze Pollution Act (2014)** allows companies anywhere to be fined for causing haze that affects Singapore.\n\nThe **Meteorological Service Singapore (MSS)** issues forecasts, heavy-rain and lightning warnings; PUB monitors drains and canals for flash floods.",
      diagram: `<svg viewBox="0 0 320 210" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Sketch map of Singapore and surroundings showing the Northeast Monsoon from the South China Sea, the Southwest Monsoon from the south-west, a Sumatra squall line and haze from fires in Sumatra and Kalimantan"><defs><marker id="sg-blue" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#1d4ed8"/></marker><marker id="sg-orange" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#c2410c"/></marker></defs><rect x="0" y="0" width="320" height="210" fill="#e0f2fe"/><path d="M90,10 L150,15 L160,70 L140,95 L120,90 L110,50 Z" fill="#bbf7d0" stroke="#15803d"/><text x="130" y="45" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14532d">Peninsular</text><text x="130" y="57" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14532d">Malaysia</text><path d="M10,60 L60,90 L110,140 L120,200 L70,200 L20,130 Z" fill="#bbf7d0" stroke="#15803d"/><text x="62" y="150" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14532d">Sumatra</text><path d="M210,90 L300,80 L310,180 L230,190 L205,140 Z" fill="#bbf7d0" stroke="#15803d"/><text x="258" y="140" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14532d">Kalimantan</text><circle cx="148" cy="100" r="5" fill="#dc2626"/><text x="158" y="104" font-size="10" fill="#7f1d1d" font-weight="bold">Singapore</text><line x1="280" y1="20" x2="170" y2="85" stroke="#1d4ed8" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#sg-blue)"/><text x="205" y="22" font-size="10" fill="#1e3a8a">NE Monsoon (Dec–Mar)</text><line x1="40" y1="200" x2="130" y2="115" stroke="#1d4ed8" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#sg-blue)"/><text x="125" y="180" font-size="10" fill="#1e3a8a">SW Monsoon</text><text x="125" y="192" font-size="10" fill="#1e3a8a">(Jun–Sep)</text><path d="M95,70 Q100,95 110,120" fill="none" stroke="#7c3aed" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="40" y="78" font-size="9" fill="#5b21b6">squall line</text><circle cx="80" cy="165" r="6" fill="#f97316"/><circle cx="240" cy="160" r="6" fill="#f97316"/><line x1="88" y1="160" x2="135" y2="110" stroke="#c2410c" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3 2" marker-end="url(#sg-orange)"/><text x="160" y="160" font-size="9" fill="#9a3412">fires → haze</text><text x="5" y="20" font-size="9" fill="#0f172a">Not to scale</text></svg>`,
      diagramCaption:
        "Sketch map (not to scale): the NE Monsoon blows from the South China Sea; the SW Monsoon blows from the Sumatra side, bringing squalls before dawn and, in fire seasons, haze from Indonesia.",
